def servo-connector
  title "Hobby servo connector with bulk capacitor"
  about "A 3-pin 2.54 mm header for a hobby servo – pin 1 signal, pin 2 +5 V, pin 3 GND – and 1000 µF across +5 V and
      GND at the connector, which supplies the servo's start and stall current peaks close to the servo instead of
      through the board's supply path."
  port sig "the servo's control signal (a PWM-capable GPIO)"
  port vservo "the servo's supply, typically 5 V (4.8–6 V servos)"
  port gnd "ground"
  part J1 connector PZ254V-11-03P lcsc C2937625 "{as}: servo (1 signal, 2 +, 3 GND)"
    notes "Pin 1 = signal, pin 2 = + (the middle), pin 3 = GND. Through-hole, hand-soldered at JLCPCB."
  part C1 capacitor RVT1C102M1010 lcsc C3359 value 1000uF "{as}: 1000 µF at the servo connector"
    notes "Electrolytic, pin 1 = plus. Directly beside J1."
  net sig J1.1
  net vservo J1.2 C1.plus
  net gnd J1.3 C1.minus
  covers HINT.bulk LIM.i LIM.v
  notes "Pin order: assumption – the usual order of hobby-servo leads (signal, + in the middle, ground) was not read
      in a servo data sheet here; check the servo's. With + in the middle a plug turned round swaps only signal and
      ground."
  notes "The servo's current: give the load in sim.json as an external current on J1 (plus \"2\", minus \"3\") – its
      running current, and its stall current as a fault case (\"matrix\": false); maker-coverage MOT 16 names 2.5 A
      for an MG996R at stall (not read here). J1 carries 3 A (XFCN drawing): LIM.i holds it."
  notes "1000 µF / 16 V: design choice; the servo data sheets name no capacitor. 1 mF gives a 1 A, 1 ms current peak a
      sag of 1 V before the supply path takes over (ΔV = I·t / C, ESR not given). Rated ≥ 1.5 × 6 V. Ripple current
      347 mA at 120 Hz (C3359): a servo's 50 Hz current pulses can exceed it – a larger or second capacitor for big
      servos."
  notes "Supply: a servo on the MCU's rail resets it when it starts or stalls (maker-coverage MOT 16, FT-1) – give
      vservo its own regulator or supply, joined at ground. USB alone gives 500 mA (HINT.usb_budget)."
  notes "The signal: a GPIO directly; on a long cable a series resistor at the MCU protects the pin (HINT.cable_line).
      Whether a 3.3 V signal is enough for a 5 V servo, its data sheet says – none was read here."
  set test {
      "connect": {"sig": "SIG", "vservo": "+5V"},
      "sim": {
        "loads": {
          "t_servo": {
            "about": "block test: a servo holding its position, 0.2 A at J2 (2 → 3), LEDs off – within the example's 0.5 A USB source",
            "gpio": {"LED_OE": 1},
            "leds_on": [],
            "external": {"J2": {"plus": "2", "minus": "3", "i_a": 0.2}}}}}}
